<section class="block-section do">
  <span class="section-tag">Do</span>
  <h2>Today's work: everyone does this</h2>
  <figure class="content-figure">
    <img src="/assets/img/u2/u2-chennai-harbor-s2cloudless-2016.jpg" alt="Satellite image of the Chennai coast from about 13.03 N to 13.17 N, north up. Marina Beach is the wide sand at the bottom, the Cooum mouth is just above it, Chennai Port is in the middle, and a narrow, groyne-lined shore runs north from the fishing harbor.">
    <figcaption>The Chennai coast, north up. Sentinel-2 cloudless, <a href="https://s2maps.eu" target="_blank" rel="noopener">https://s2maps.eu</a>, by EOX IT Services GmbH (Contains modified Copernicus Sentinel data 2016 &amp; 2017), CC BY 4.0. Limitation: this is a mosaic of the clearest scenes from 2016 and 2017, so it has no single date.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <div class="callout task">
    <div class="callout-title">The task: Read the harbor</div>
    <p>On the satellite image, find where the beach is widest and where the shore is narrowest. Draw one arrow for the direction sand moves along this coast. In two sentences, explain what the harbor walls did to the sand. Then open Google Earth, pick one other structure built out from the Chennai coast, and say which side of it has gained sand.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="callout support">
    <div class="callout-title">If you want support</div>
    <p>Waves reach the beach at an angle, so each swash carries sand up the beach on a slant and the backwash pulls it straight back down. Over thousands of waves the sand walks along the coast: longshore drift. Chennai's harbor breakwaters block that walk, which is why Marina, to the south, is wide and the shore to the north erodes.</p>
    <figure class="content-figure">
      <img src="/assets/img/u2/u2-earle-17-1-8-longshore-drift.png" alt="Diagram of a breaking wave meeting a sandy beach at an angle. Arrows trace one sand grain's path: up the beach on a slant with the swash, straight back down with the backwash, repeated in a zigzag that moves the grain along the shore.">
      <figcaption>Figure 17.1.8, the movement of particles on a beach by swash and backwash. © Steven Earle, <a href="https://opentextbc.ca/physicalgeology2ed/chapter/17-1-waves/" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><em>Physical Geology</em>, 2nd ed.</a>, CC BY 4.0.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p><a href="https://www.ck12.org/earth-science/Landforms-from-Wave-Erosion-and-Deposition/lesson/Landforms-from-Wave-Erosion-and-Deposition-HS-ES/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Easier reading: CK-12, Landforms from Wave Erosion and Deposition</a>. Beaches, spits and sea cliffs at a gentler reading level. Pairs with Earle 17.2 and 17.3.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="callout extend">
    <div class="callout-title">If you want to go further</div>
    <p>Compare this coast with Mahabalipuram, using the Shore Temple as your marker.</p>
  </div>
